  "slug": "confidence-ordering-contextual-68b5",
  "summary": "Simulation claims about RNA ensembles, protein communication and dsRNA unwinding increasingly need explicit reference baselines to be judged.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a concern with validating simulation-derived claims against an explicit reference. mdRNAcat provides a curated all-atom REST2 replica-exchange MD dataset of 48 RNA systems, with coordinates and reference-Hamiltonian forces in standardized HDF5, offering a common basis for checking conformational ensemble claims. The quantum-walk study applies Shannon entropy on protein contact networks to predict long-range communication residues and is validated on AsLOV2. The DDX3X study combines coarse-grained simulations with deep learning to describe ATP-independent, entropy-driven dsRNA unwinding via rare strand-displacing intermediates. This benchmark orientation is dominant rather than universal, since the quantum-walk and DDX3X studies are less directly benchmark-oriented. What is new is the explicit reference-Hamiltonian control in the mdRNAcat resource, against which ensemble and force claims can be measured.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-31"
}
